WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.280
In 2012, Facebook faced a serious engineering problem.

2
00:00:04.280 --> 00:00:06.720
The consumer internet was shifting to mobile,

3
00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:09.880
but the hardware landscape was incredibly fragmented.

4
00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:12.480
Building entirely separate native code bases

5
00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:16.120
for thousands of distinct devices was a logistical nightmare,

6
00:00:16.120 --> 00:00:19.280
so Facebook relied on an HTML5 mobile web app

7
00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:21.040
to deliver its newsfeed.

8
00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:23.400
The performance was painfully slow.

9
00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:25.400
Engineers attempted a compromise.

10
00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:27.400
They took their existing web application

11
00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:31.080
and wrapped it inside a native mobile container, a web view,

12
00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:33.000
hoping to extract native performance

13
00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:34.880
without rewriting the code base.

14
00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:37.480
The result remained sluggish and felt out of place

15
00:00:37.480 --> 00:00:38.680
on a smartphone.

16
00:00:38.680 --> 00:00:40.560
Development teams across the industry

17
00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:42.720
were trapped in a strict dichotomy.

18
00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:44.800
They could either build for the web, allowing them

19
00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:47.160
to iterate quickly and deploy instantly,

20
00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:50.160
or they could spend the time and money writing custom native code

21
00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:53.600
for iOS and Android to achieve fluid performance.

22
00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:55.800
Choosing between developer velocity

23
00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:59.600
and a responsive user experience was a zero-sum game,

24
00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:02.640
creating an existential threat for major platforms

25
00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:05.600
that relied on high-speed user engagement.

26
00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:08.280
During a 2013 Facebook hackathon,

27
00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:10.440
engineers Jordan Walk, who had already

28
00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:13.440
built React for the web, and Christopher Chodot,

29
00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:16.280
started experimenting with a completely different approach

30
00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:18.360
to mobile user interfaces.

31
00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:20.720
They arrived at a structural insight.

32
00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:24.440
They could write the application's logic entirely in JavaScript,

33
00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:27.040
but instruct the device's native rendering layer

34
00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:29.400
to draw the actual visual elements.

35
00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:32.560
This experiment evolved into React native.

36
00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:36.080
At the 2015 F8 conference, Facebook open sourced

37
00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:39.680
the framework, operating under a highly appealing philosophy,

38
00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:41.960
learn once, write anywhere.

39
00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:45.080
The engineering community adopted it immediately.

40
00:01:45.080 --> 00:01:48.200
Startups and tech giants alike integrated the framework

41
00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:50.000
into their production environments,

42
00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:53.120
with companies like Microsoft, Shopify, Discord,

43
00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:56.080
and Walmart building highly complex applications

44
00:01:56.080 --> 00:01:57.720
on the new system.

45
00:01:57.720 --> 00:02:01.240
By separating the underlying logic from the rendering process,

46
00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:04.320
React native gave engineers the rapid iteration speeds

47
00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:07.400
of web development while outputting genuine native UI

48
00:02:07.400 --> 00:02:11.160
elements, like an actual U-button on an iPhone.

49
00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:13.320
The engine that made this separation possible

50
00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:16.360
was a system known internally as the bridge.

51
00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:20.160
This diagram illustrates the mechanical flaw in that system.

52
00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:22.800
Every single interaction, drawing a pixel,

53
00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:25.960
responding to a swipe, or measuring a block of text,

54
00:02:25.960 --> 00:02:29.040
had to be serialized into a JSON text message,

55
00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:32.680
sent across a divide, and decoded by the native side.

56
00:02:32.680 --> 00:02:35.560
Because this communication was strictly asynchronous,

57
00:02:35.560 --> 00:02:39.240
it was mathematically impossible to call native code synchronously.

58
00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:42.720
Even a simple request to read the width of a layout box

59
00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:45.080
meant sending a message across the bridge

60
00:02:45.080 --> 00:02:47.720
and waiting for a callback, while other messages

61
00:02:47.720 --> 00:02:49.600
were piling up in a queue.

62
00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:52.680
As applications grew larger and user interfaces

63
00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:56.120
demanded higher frame rates, this original workaround

64
00:02:56.120 --> 00:02:59.360
devolved into a severe structural bottleneck.

65
00:02:59.360 --> 00:03:01.800
Users felt this bottleneck directly.

66
00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:05.200
High-frequency operations like complex screen transitions

67
00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:09.040
or scroll-driven animations would stutter and drop frames,

68
00:03:09.040 --> 00:03:12.600
imposing a hard ceiling on application performance.

69
00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:15.800
This legacy structure was also completely incompatible

70
00:03:15.800 --> 00:03:17.680
with newer web advancements.

71
00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:20.000
Features like React's concurrent mode,

72
00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:21.720
which allow the framework to pause

73
00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:24.360
and prioritize rendering tasks in the background,

74
00:03:24.360 --> 00:03:28.360
simply could not function over a slow asynchronous bridge.

75
00:03:28.360 --> 00:03:31.040
Rather than trying to patch the system again,

76
00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:35.160
meta and Microsoft made a drastic decision in 2022.

77
00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:36.960
They chose to abandon the bridge

78
00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:41.000
and initiate a total rewrite of the frameworks internal foundation.

79
00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:43.360
The engineering stakes were massive.

80
00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:46.080
They had to rip out and rebuild the core engine

81
00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:48.640
of the most popular mobile framework in the world,

82
00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:50.520
entirely behind the scenes.

83
00:03:50.520 --> 00:03:53.120
Without breaking the millions of active applications

84
00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:54.560
relying on it.

85
00:03:54.560 --> 00:03:57.200
After years of incremental internal work,

86
00:03:57.200 --> 00:04:00.080
this rewrite, known as the new architecture,

87
00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:02.640
shipped as the default standard in React Native

88
00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:06.640
version 0.76 in October 2024.

89
00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:09.240
This diagram outlines the new mechanics.

90
00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:13.040
The first pillar is JSI or the JavaScript interface.

91
00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:15.960
It eliminates JSON serialization completely,

92
00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:18.000
creating direct synchronous bindings

93
00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:21.400
between JavaScript and C++ memory spaces.

94
00:04:21.400 --> 00:04:23.200
The second pillar is Fabric.

95
00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:25.280
By bypassing the old bottleneck,

96
00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:27.720
this new shared C++ rendering engine

97
00:04:27.720 --> 00:04:29.920
handles layout measurements synchronously

98
00:04:29.920 --> 00:04:32.960
and enables React to process UI rendering concurrently

99
00:04:32.960 --> 00:04:35.240
without freezing the main device thread.

100
00:04:35.240 --> 00:04:37.320
The third pillar is Turbo Modules.

101
00:04:37.320 --> 00:04:40.120
Instead of forcing an application to initialize

102
00:04:40.120 --> 00:04:43.320
every native feature, like Bluetooth or the camera,

103
00:04:43.320 --> 00:04:46.960
during startup, Turbo Modules lazily load these tools

104
00:04:46.960 --> 00:04:49.360
only at the exact moment they are requested

105
00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:52.640
by the JavaScript layer, cutting down startup times.

106
00:04:52.640 --> 00:04:55.560
These three pillars allow React Native to operate

107
00:04:55.560 --> 00:04:58.880
as a low-level engine, removing the need for a bridge

108
00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:01.000
and integrating the logic layer directly

109
00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:02.600
into the native runtime.

110
00:05:02.600 --> 00:05:05.480
Today, complex, gesture-driven animations

111
00:05:05.480 --> 00:05:09.520
run at 120 frames per second directly on the UI thread.

112
00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:12.280
The performance gap between React Native and pure native

113
00:05:12.280 --> 00:05:15.640
development is functionally negligible for most applications,

114
00:05:15.640 --> 00:05:17.560
supported by tools like Expo.

115
00:05:17.560 --> 00:05:21.240
The framework is now used by Shopify, Meta and Microsoft

116
00:05:21.240 --> 00:05:23.400
to power their flagship applications,

117
00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:25.160
providing a standardized environment

118
00:05:25.160 --> 00:05:27.240
for production scale mobile code.

119
00:05:27.240 --> 00:05:29.320
10 years after the first prototype,

120
00:05:29.320 --> 00:05:31.760
the framework delivers the speed of web development

121
00:05:31.760 --> 00:05:34.600
alongside the execution of native mobile software.

